---
import TableScroll from '../../../components/TableScroll.astro';
import PtosGuide from '../../../components/PtosGuide.astro';

const example = [
  '; Save as PTOS.INI in the root of the boot drive.',
  '[binfmt.tos68k]',
  'enabled = true',
  'offset = 0',
  'magic = 601A',
  'mask = FFFF',
  'interpreter = \\BIN\\PARATOS.TOS',
].join('\n');
---
<PtosGuide title="PTOS.INI" description="Register executable format handlers on the boot drive." sources={[
  ['ptos', 'doc/binfmt.txt', 'Executable interpreter syntax, ordering and limits'],
  ['ptos', 'bdos/binfmt.c', 'Boot-drive lookup and executable loading'],
  ['ptos', 'bdos/binfmt_rules.c', 'Configuration parser'],
  ['ptos', 'bios/bios.c', 'Boot order'],
  ['ptos', 'bdos/Kconfig', 'CONF_WITH_BINFMT'],
]}>
  <h2 class="prm-section-heading ruled">Location and boot order</h2>
  <p>Place <code>PTOS.INI</code> in the root of the drive that pTOS boots from, for example <code>C:\PTOS.INI</code>.
    On a build with <code>CONF_WITH_BINFMT</code>, pTOS reads it once after setting up the filesystem and default environment
    and before starting the early console or running <code>AUTO</code> programs. Changes take effect at the next boot.</p>
  <p>A missing file is allowed. An unreadable file or one larger than 64 KiB leaves the configured handler list empty and is reported on the console.
    The build option also enables <code>#!</code> scripts, which can run without an INI file.</p>

  <h2 class="prm-section-heading ruled">File format</h2>
  <p>Use LF or CRLF line endings and one <code>key = value</code> per line. Whole-line comments start with <code>;</code> or <code>#</code>.
    Section names and keys are ASCII case-insensitive. Values are literal, unquoted text. Paths may contain spaces and can use
    either slash. The parser accepts up to eight handler definitions and 512 bytes per line, excluding the line ending.</p>
  <pre class="prm-code"><code>{example}</code></pre>
  <p>This rule selects a native ParaTOS executable for a file with a 68k PRG header when a native loader has not claimed the file.
    Install the target’s ParaTOS executable at the path in the example, or substitute the absolute path to your interpreter.</p>

  <TableScroll label="PTOS.INI executable handler keys">
    <table class="prm-rule-table prm-key-table">
      <caption>Keys in <code>[binfmt.NAME]</code></caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">Value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>enabled</code></th><td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code>. Defaults to <code>true</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>offset</code></th><td>Unsigned byte offset, decimal or prefixed with <code>0x</code>. Defaults to 0.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>magic</code></th><td>Required sequence of one to 32 hex bytes. Omit the <code>0x</code> prefix.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>mask</code></th><td>Hex bytes of the same length as <code>magic</code>. Defaults to <code>FF</code> for every byte.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>interpreter</code></th><td>Required absolute path to the interpreter. <code>\BIN\APP.TOS</code> is relative to the boot drive’s root. <code>D:\BIN\APP.TOS</code> names another drive explicitly.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>Use letters, digits, underscores or hyphens in <code>NAME</code>. Invalid handlers are skipped individually.
    Unknown sections are ignored. Unknown keys are diagnosed, and a duplicate key invalidates its handler.</p>

  <h2 class="prm-section-heading ruled">Handler selection</h2>
  <p>Native executable recognition takes precedence, followed by a <code>#!</code> line and then enabled INI rules in file order.
    A rule matches when every masked byte at the given offset equals the corresponding masked byte in <code>magic</code>.
    Malformed native executables and errors from a selected interpreter propagate to the caller.
    The selected interpreter receives the original program path followed by its arguments.
    For a script, the optional argument on the <code>#!</code> line precedes the script path.</p>
  <p>Native <a href="/reference/pexecve/">Pexecve</a> preserves argument boundaries through each redirect.
    For a direct PRG redirect, ParaTOS can read the original name and raw guest data from
    <a href="/os-variants/ptos/elf-entry/#guest-launch"><code>AT_PTOS_GUEST_LAUNCH</code></a>.</p>
  <p>For the system font setting, see <a href="/os-variants/ptos/fonts/#system-font">PTOS.INF under Font installation</a>.</p>
</PtosGuide>
